'''Varakí''' is the largest city and administrative capital of the [[Cumará (comarca)|Cumará comarca]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 852,362 in 2026, it is the principal commuter city of the southern Estret corridor, feeding workers into both [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[International City of Areza]]. The city sits on the shore of [[Lake Jarumá]], a natural lake north of the International City of Areza, and its lakefront apartment towers and transit-oriented development make it the densest municipality in Cumará.

Varakí's growth was driven by three successive waves: its origins as the last railroad stop on the Zong-era central line, the construction boom of the 1960s and 1970s when workers flooded the area to build the new International City of Areza, and the real estate bubble of the 1980s which produced the lakefront tower skyline that defines the city today. The addition of a high-speed rail stop in 2008 cemented Varakí's role as a dual-commuter city, uniquely positioned between the national capital and the international district.

== Geography ==

[[File:Varaki lakefront towers.png|thumb|300px|Lakefront apartment towers along Lake Jarumá in Zona 1]]

Varakí occupies a broad, gently sloping strip of land along the shore of [[Lake Jarumá]], a natural lake situated north of the [[International City of Areza]]. Lake Jarumá and [[Lake Alvarado]] — the artificial lake created by the damming of the [[Balboa Canal]] corridor to the south — flank Areza on either side, with the international city bounded between the two lakes. The city's western edge follows the lakefront, where the highest-density residential development is concentrated, while the terrain rises gradually toward the east into the foothills of the [[Serralada d'Estret]]. The city covers 200.1 km² and has a population density of approximately 4,260 per km², the highest in the comarca.

The lakefront is the city's defining geographic feature. The shore of Lake Jarumá at Varakí is relatively straight, with a narrow band of land between the water's edge and the rising ground behind it. This strip — now lined with apartment towers, promenades, and the lakefront streetcar line — was the focus of the 1980s real estate boom and remains the most desirable residential area in the city.

The eastern neighbourhoods climb into the lower slopes of the Serralada d'Estret, where the terrain becomes hillier and the development pattern shifts from dense apartment blocks to lower-density residential streets. The [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] Line 9 corridor runs through these eastern neighbourhoods, following the canal corridor alignment between Sant Cristòfor and Areza.

=== Climate ===

Varakí has a {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} ({{w|Köppen climate classification|Köppen}} Am), typical of the southern Estret lowlands. Temperatures are consistently warm year-round, with average daily highs of 30–32 °C and lows of 22–24 °C. The wet season runs from May to November, with the heaviest rainfall from June to August. The lakefront position moderates temperatures slightly compared to the inland comarca, and the rising terrain to the east can produce localised afternoon thunderstorms during the wet season.

== History ==

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

[[File:Varaki railway station historical.png|thumb|300px|The original Zong-era railway station at Varakí, c. 1930]]

Varakí originated as a small lakeside settlement at the southern terminus of the Zong-era central railway line. During the [[Zong Balboa]] period, the railway was extended southward from Sant Cristòfor through the Cumará comarca toward the canal zone, and Varakí — whose name derives from the [[Birú language|Birú]] ''warakí'' ("peaceful water"), referring to the calm waters of the lake — was established as the last stop before the canal corridor. The settlement remained small throughout the Zong period, consisting of a railway station, a cluster of workers' housing, and a few lakeside fishing families. The Zong administration's territorial expansion had absorbed Cumará into the greater Sant Cristòfor administrative district, but Varakí itself was little more than a railhead.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) largely bypassed Varakí, which was too small and too far from the fighting to be affected. The railway line was maintained for military logistics, and the station remained operational throughout the war.

=== AC mandate and Areza construction boom (1950–1973) ===

The [[Adonian Community]] mandate period transformed Varakí's fortunes. In 1950, the AC assumed administration of the former Zong territory, and planning began for the [[International City of Areza]] — a new permanent international district on the shore of Lake Alvarado, south of Lake Jarumá. The construction of Areza, which began in earnest in the late 1950s and continued through the 1960s, drew thousands of workers to the southern Estret corridor. Varakí, situated on the opposite shore of Lake Jarumá to the north and already connected by rail to Sant Cristòfor, became the natural residential base for the construction workforce.

The population swelled rapidly. Informal settlements and worker housing spread outward from the old railway station, and the municipal government — established in 1961 when the post-reunification territorial reorganisation separated Cumará from Sant Cristòfor — struggled to keep pace with the growth. The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973), which transferred the canal zone to Balboan sovereignty and completed the reunification of Estret Province, further accelerated development by opening the corridor to private investment.

=== Real estate bubble and lakefront transformation (1973–2000) ===

[[File:Varaki lakefront construction boom.png|thumb|300px|Lakefront tower construction during the 1980s real estate boom]]

The post-reunification period brought a sustained real estate boom to the southern Estret corridor. Varakí's lakefront position, its rail connection to both Sant Cristòfor and Areza, and the growing demand for commuter housing combined to produce a speculative construction boom in the 1980s. Apartment towers — many of them built on the narrow strip of reclaimed land along the lakefront — rose rapidly, transforming the city's skyline from a low-rise railhead into a dense residential wall of towers. The boom was fuelled by a combination of domestic investment, returning Balboan capital, and foreign speculation tied to the Areza development.

The bubble burst in the early 1990s, leaving a number of unfinished towers and overextended developers. However, the physical transformation of the lakefront was permanent, and the completed towers were gradually absorbed by the city's growing commuter population. By the late 1990s, Varakí had established itself as the principal commuter city of the southern corridor, with a population approaching 600,000.

=== Metro extension and HSR stop (2000–present) ===

[[File:Varaki HSR station.png|thumb|300px|The Varakí high-speed rail station on Avinguda Central]]

The opening of [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] Line 9 in 2003, which connected Sant Cristòfor to Areza via the canal corridor through the eastern neighbourhoods of Varakí, provided the city with a rapid transit link to both destinations. The line's 10 stations within Varakí made it the primary internal circulation system for the eastern half of the city.

In 2008, a high-speed rail stop was added at Varakí on the [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Areza high-speed rail line]]. The stop, which serves a selection of HSR services before they enter Areza, gave Varakí residents a direct connection to the national capital in under 15 minutes and to Areza in under 10. The HSR stop has been a significant driver of further residential development and has reinforced Varakí's dual-commuter identity.

The city's population surpassed 850,000 in the 2020s, making it one of the largest cities in Estret Province outside Sant Cristòfor itself.

== Government ==

Varakí is governed by an elected mayor and a municipal council. The mayor serves four-year terms and has executive authority over municipal administration, planning, and local services. The current mayor is Francesc Salvà i Ousmane of the [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] (PLB), elected in 2024. Salvà i Ousmane, a former municipal planner from Zona 3, campaigned on transit integration and lakefront regulation, appealing to the city's dual-commuter population and the growing professional class in Zona 4. The municipal council is composed of councillors elected from the city's five zones.

As the comarca capital, Varakí also hosts the comarca-level government offices for Cumará.

== Demographics ==

Varakí's population of 852,362 (2026) makes it the largest municipality in the Cumará comarca and one of the largest in Estret Province outside Sant Cristòfor. The city's population is predominantly commuter-oriented, with a large proportion of working-age residents who commute daily to Sant Cristòfor or Areza.

The ethnic composition reflects the comarca's suburban character and the successive waves of migration that shaped the city. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] constitute the largest group, concentrated in the older neighbourhoods near the original railway station and the western housing estates. Mixed Balboans are distributed throughout the city, particularly in the lakefront apartment towers. White Balboans are present in the newer lakefront developments and the higher-income eastern neighbourhoods. The [[Fosian Balboan|Fosian]] community, including both [[Sinchew]]ese and [[Zong Balboan|Zong Balboans]], is concentrated in the commercial districts near the HSR station and the older neighbourhoods along the railway line.

The city's population is younger than the Estret average, reflecting the influx of working-age commuters, and the household size is smaller than the national average, consistent with the high proportion of apartment-dwelling single professionals and young families.

== Economy ==

Varakí's economy is based on commuting, residential services, retail, and local commerce. The city functions primarily as a bedroom community for both Sant Cristòfor and Areza, with a large proportion of the working population commuting daily by rail or metro. The HSR stop, added in 2008, has attracted a growing number of Areza-based professionals who live in Varakí for its lower housing costs and lakefront amenities.

The lakefront commercial strip — restaurants, cafés, retail, and service businesses — is the city's principal commercial district, serving both the local population and weekend visitors from Sant Cristòfor. The area around the HSR station has developed as a secondary commercial node, with office space, hotels, and conference facilities catering to the Areza commuter market.

Local industry is limited, consisting primarily of small-scale manufacturing, logistics, and construction-related businesses clustered along the railway corridor and the canal road. The city's agricultural fringe, in the eastern and southern neighbourhoods where the terrain rises toward the Serralada, supports small-scale farming and market gardens.

== Transport ==

Varakí's transport network is built around its dual-commuter role, with connections to both Sant Cristòfor and Areza. The system integrates with the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor metro]] and the national rail network while maintaining its own municipal streetcar and bus services.

=== High-speed rail ===

The [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Areza high-speed rail line]], operated by [[Intercon]], passes through Varakí. A stop was added in 2008 at the [[Varakí TDV (CristoCarril)|Varakí TDV]] station, an eight-track, four-platform interchange on Avinguda Central in Zona 3. The station was built as part of the HSR project to relieve the capacity constraints of the older [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]], which has only two platforms with passing tracks. The stop is served by a selection of HSR services — not all trains stop at Varakí, with express services bypassing the city on dedicated through tracks. The journey time to Sant Cristòfor is approximately 12 minutes, and to Areza approximately 8 minutes. The HSR stop has made Varakí one of the most accessible commuter cities in the Estret corridor.

=== Commuter rail ===

[[CristoCarril]] [[Line C (CristoCarril)|Line C]], operated by [[Intercon]], terminates at [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]] in the western part of the city, following the alignment of the original Zong-era central railway line northward through [[La Cruïlla]] and the eastern ciutats of [[Sant Cristòfor]] to the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Central Terminal]]. The line serves two stations within Varakí: [[Varakí TDV (CristoCarril)|Varakí TDV]], an eight-track interchange station on Avinguda Central that connects to the [[Sant Cristòfor–Portnou HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Portnou]] and [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Areza]] high-speed rail lines; and [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]], the city's older principal rail hub, which has only two platforms with passing tracks. After arriving at Varakí Central, Line C services turn back northward to Varakí TDV, where they dwell on terminal tracks before being dispatched for their next working. Between Varakí TDV and Varakí Central, the line shares tracks with [[Rodalies de Portnou]] Line 4, which continues south to the [[Portnou]] metropolitan area. Varakí TDV also houses a train yard serving both the high-speed rail fleet and CristoCarril Line C rolling stock. Line C provides a slower but more frequent alternative to the HSR for daily commuters, with three service patterns (short, express, and local) offering varying levels of coverage along the 65.7 km route.

=== Metro ===

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] Line 9 crosses the eastern neighbourhoods of Varakí, following the canal corridor alignment between Sant Cristòfor and Areza. The line opened in 2003 and has 10 stations within Varakí, making it the primary rapid transit service for the eastern half of the city. Line 9 connects Varakí to [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]], the Sant Cristòfor metro network, and the [[Areza Metro]] at its southern terminus. The line carries approximately 58,000 passengers daily across its full length, with a significant proportion of ridership originating in Varakí.

=== Streetcar ===

[[File:Varaki streetcar Line A lakefront.png|thumb|300px|A Varakí streetcar on Line A along the Lake Jarumá promenade]]

Varakí operates a three-line streetcar system, the Tramvia de Varakí, managed by the municipal transport authority. The system provides internal circulation within the city, complementing the through-running commuter rail and metro services.

