The streetcar fleet consists of modern low-floor LRVs. Fares are integrated with the national [[Tarifa Integrada]] system where applicable, though the Varakí streetcar operates under a separate municipal fare structure.

=== Bus ===

The city bus network, operated by the municipal transport authority, complements the rail and streetcar services with surface routes covering the entire city. The bus network provides feeder connections to the metro, commuter rail, and HSR stations, and serves the lower-density eastern and southern neighbourhoods where the streetcar and metro do not reach. Bus routes also connect Varakí to the surrounding municipalities in the Cumará comarca, including [[La Cruïlla]], [[Enllaç]], and [[Mas Vell]].

== Neighbourhoods ==

Varakí is divided into five municipal zones, each reflecting a different period of the city's growth:

=== Zona 1 — Lakefront ===

[[File:Varaki Zona 1 lakefront promenade.png|thumb|300px|The Lake Jarumá promenade in Zona 1, with apartment towers behind]]

The oldest part of the city, Zona 1 began as a small [[Birú people|Birú]] fishing settlement on the shore of Lake Jarumá. The original village was absorbed by the Zong-era railway development, but the shore itself remained a fishing and boat-building area well into the post-reunification period. The 1980s real estate boom transformed the lakefront into the city's primary commercial and residential corridor: the fishing shacks and boatyards were replaced by a promenade, apartment towers, restaurants, and retail. The lakefront promenade is now the city's social and commercial heart, and Streetcar Line A runs along its full length. Despite the transformation, the street pattern of the original fishing village survives in the narrow lanes behind the tower frontage.

=== Zona 2 — Western ===

[[File:Varaki Zona 2 Zong era row houses.png|thumb|300px|Zong-era row houses in Zona 2]]

Zona 2 was built during the Zong Balboa period as commuter suburbs for railway workers and junior staff stationed at the canal zone. The housing stock consists predominantly of Zong-era row houses and low-rise apartment blocks, laid out in the grid pattern typical of Zong-era planned settlements. The area retains a working-class character, and the CristoCarril Line C commuter rail runs along its western edge, following the alignment of the original Zong-era central railway line. Zona 2 is the most ethnically diverse part of the city, with a large [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboan]] population and a significant [[Fosian Balboan|Fosian]] community dating to the Zong period. Residents of Zona 2 predominantly commute to Sant Cristòfor via CristoCarril Line C, which follows the original Zong-era railway alignment northward to the Central Terminal.

=== Zona 3 — Central ===

Zona 3, located between the lakefront and the western districts, was developed in the 1970s and 1980s as the city's population grew during the Areza construction and post-reunification boom. The area is sleepier and more suburban in character than Zona 1, with a mix of mid-rise apartment blocks, townhouses, and garden apartments. Zona 3 contains the municipal government offices, the comarca administration, and the original railway station. Avinguda Central, the city's main boulevard, runs through Zona 3, and Streetcar Line C follows it to connect the HSR station to the Metro Line 9 corridor.

=== Zona 4 — Eastern ===

Zona 4 was developed in the 1990s and 2000s, coinciding with the construction of Metro Line 9 through the canal corridor. The development is newer and more varied in density than the older zones, with a mix of mid-rise apartment blocks, townhouses, and lower-density residential streets climbing the lower slopes of the Serralada d'Estret. The 10 Metro Line 9 stations within Varakí serve as the primary transport nodes for Zona 4, and the area has attracted a younger, more affluent commuter population drawn by the direct metro access to Areza. Unlike Zona 2, which commutes predominantly to Sant Cristòfor, residents of Zona 4 generally commute southward to the International City via Metro Line 9, which connects directly to the Areza Metro at its southern terminus.

=== Zona 5 — Far Western ===

[[File:Varaki Zona 5 industrial district.png|thumb|300px|Food processing facilities in Zona 5]]

Zona 5 is the far western edge of the city, an industrial and working-class district distinct from the Zong-era commuter suburbs of Zona 2 to its east. The area developed as an industrial fringe during the post-reunification period, and its primary economic activity is food processing — fish packing, milling, and light manufacturing — taking advantage of the rail connection and the proximity to the canal corridor road. The housing stock is predominantly low-rise workers' housing and industrial-era apartment blocks. Zona 5 is the least residential of the five zones and the only part of the city with a significant industrial base.

== Notable places ==

* [[Varakí TDV (CristoCarril)|Varakí TDV station]] — the high-speed rail stop on the Sant Cristòfor–Areza line, opened 2008, with eight tracks, four island platforms, and an adjacent train yard
* [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]] — the city's older principal rail station, terminus of CristoCarril Line C, located on the site of the original Zong-era railway station, with two platforms and passing tracks
* Isthmus Railway turntable — the original turntable for the Zong-era central line terminus, preserved as a heritage site near the old railway station in Zona 2. The turntable was used to reverse locomotives at the end of the line before the railway was extended further south, and is one of the few surviving Zong-era railway structures in the southern corridor.
* Cathedral of Jarumá — the fourth-largest cathedral in Balboa, located in Zona 3 near the municipal government offices. The cathedral serves as the principal [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] place of worship for the Cumará comarca.

[[File:Cathedral of Jaruma Varaki.png|thumb|300px|The Cathedral of Jarumá in Zona 3]]

* Lake Jarumá lakefront promenade — the waterfront promenade along the Zona 1 tower district
* Avinguda Central — the city's main boulevard, connecting the HSR station to the Metro Line 9 corridor

